.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-SET-MESSAGE-VISIBILITY 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-set-message-visibility \- set a message's visibility timeout
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs set-visibility
.BR \-\-message\-id | \-m
.I MESSAGE-ID
.BR \-\-visibility | \-v
.I SECONDS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the visibility timeout for an individual message that was
previously received via
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1),
extending or shortening how long it stays invisible to other receivers
before it can be received again.
.PP
To change the figure every receive starts from, rather than this one
message, use
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-set-queue-visibility (1).
.PP
This action was called
.B set-visibility
before its queue-level counterpart existed. That name still works, both
on the command line and as an
.I x-euclid-action
- euclid-jdk sends it - but the two-word spelling is the one that says
which visibility it changes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-message\-id " " \fIMESSAGE-ID\fR
ID of the message to update. Required.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-visibility " " \fISECONDS\fR
New visibility timeout, in seconds. Defaults to \fI10\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message's visibility timeout was updated. No output is printed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or no
message with that ID exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs set-visibility \e
    --message-id 6f1b1e2e-... --visibility 60
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-set-queue-visibility (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message-metadata (1)
